R-TG1 R-TG2 R-TG3 R-TG4 R-LD5 R-BS1 R-P4 R-EC2 R-EC19 R-EC10 R-LD7 (slice 054): the build target of one app project file. `update pf` on 10 projects that move the platform acme/<repo> from 0.1.0 to 0.1.1 (anchored by `=0.1.1` in `talon.toml`, so no other tuple exists, R-PH13). The fake machine is linux x64 (the host targets are x64musl, then x64glibc). Runs 1 to 6 change a header, so each runs the ladder: the baseline, the candidate check, the build. The build probe has `--target=<t>` before the file (R-LD5, R-TG2).
Run 1 (platform fpf, target wasm32, the build fails with the fixture `build-target`): the tuple is dropped and the failure text names the target (exit 10). Run 2 (wpf, one target): `--target=wasm32` (rule 3). Run 3 (mpf, targets wasm32, x64musl, x64glibc): `--target=x64musl` (rule 2). Run 4 (mpf, `[build] target = "wasm32"`): `--target=wasm32` (rule 1), and the build runs although run 3 passed: the key has the target. Run 5 (the same as run 4): the build is skipped (`build: skipped (recorded pass)`). Run 6 (gpf, wasm32 and x64glibc): `--target=x64glibc`. Run 7 (npf, wasm32 and x64mac): exit 2 after the check, the message lists the targets, nothing is written. Run 8 (`[build] target = "wasm64"`): exit 19 at the read of `talon.toml`, before any call of a probe. Run 9 (a package project file with `[build] target = "wasm32"`): the check only, no build and no `--target` (R-TG4, R-LD7). Run 10 (a package project file with `[build] target = "wasm64"`): the same as run 9, and no exit 19: R-TG4, a package has no build, so its `[build]` is not checked. The facts of the builds are in `compat.tsv` (`tree.diff`): the column `target` has wasm32, x64musl and x64glibc.
